{"id":"storage-storage-classes","corpus":"k8s","title":"Storage Classes","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Storage Classes"},{"kind":"p","text":"This document describes the concept of a StorageClass in Kubernetes. Familiarity with volumes and persistent volumes is suggested."},{"kind":"p","text":"A StorageClass provides a way for administrators to describe the classes of storage they offer. Different classes might map to quality-of-service levels, or to backup policies, or to arbitrary policies determined by the cluster administrators. Kubernetes itself is unopinionated about what classes represent."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Kubernetes concept of a storage class is similar to “profiles” in some other storage system designs."},{"kind":"h2","text":"StorageClass objects"},{"kind":"p","text":"Each StorageClass contains the fields provisioner, parameters, and reclaimPolicy, which are used when a PersistentVolume belonging to the class needs to be dynamically provisioned to satisfy a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)."},{"kind":"p","text":"The name of a StorageClass object is significant, and is how users can request a particular class. Administrators set the name and other parameters of a class when first creating StorageClass objects."},{"kind":"p","text":"As an administrator, you can specify a default StorageClass that applies to any PVCs that don't request a specific class. For more details, see the PersistentVolumeClaim concept."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here's an example of a StorageClass:"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Default StorageClass"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can mark a StorageClass as the default for your cluster. For instructions on setting the default StorageClass, see Change the default StorageClass."},{"kind":"p","text":"When a PVC does not specify a storageClassName, the default StorageClass is used."},{"kind":"p","text":"If you set the storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class annotation to true on more than one StorageClass in your cluster, and you then create a PersistentVolumeClaim with no storageClassName set, Kubernetes uses the most recently created default StorageClass."},{"kind":"p","text":"You should try to only have one StorageClass in your cluster that is marked as the default. The reason that Kubernetes allows you to have multiple default StorageClasses is to allow for seamless migration."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can create a PersistentVolumeClaim without specifying a storageClassName for the new PVC, and you can do so even when no default StorageClass exists in your cluster. In this case, the new PVC creates as you defined it, and the storageClassName of that PVC remains unset until a default becomes available."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can have a cluster without any default StorageClass. If you don't mark any StorageClass as default (and one hasn't been set for you by, for example, a cloud provider), then Kubernetes cannot apply that defaulting for PersistentVolumeClaims that need it."},{"kind":"p","text":"If or when a default StorageClass becomes available, the control plane identifies any existing PVCs without storageClassName. For the PVCs that either have an empty value for storageClassName or do not have this key, the control plane then updates those PVCs to set storageClassName to match the new default StorageClass. If you have an existing PVC where the storageClassName is \"\", and you configure a default StorageClass, then this PVC will not get updated."},{"kind":"p","text":"In order to keep binding to PVs with storageClassName set to \"\" (while a default StorageClass is present), you need to set the storageClassName of the associated PVC to \"\"."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Provisioner"},{"kind":"p","text":"Each StorageClass has a provisioner that determines what volume plugin is used for provisioning PVs. This field must be specified."},{"kind":"p","text":"You are not restricted to specifying the \"internal\" provisioners listed here (whose names are prefixed with \"kubernetes.io\" and shipped alongside Kubernetes). You can also run and specify external provisioners, which are independent programs that follow a specification defined by Kubernetes. Authors of external provisioners have full discretion over where their code lives, how the provisioner is shipped, how it needs to be run, what volume plugin it uses (including Flex), etc. The repository kubernetes-sigs/sig-storage-lib-external-provisioner houses a library for writing external provisioners that implements the bulk of the specification. Some external provisioners are listed under the repository kubernetes-sigs/sig-storage-lib-external-provisioner."},{"kind":"p","text":"For example, NFS doesn't provide an internal provisioner, but an external provisioner can be used. There are also cases when 3rd party storage vendors provide their own external provisioner."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Reclaim policy"},{"kind":"p","text":"PersistentVolumes that are dynamically created by a StorageClass will have the reclaim policy specified in the reclaimPolicy field of the class, which can be either Delete or Retain. If no reclaimPolicy is specified when a StorageClass object is created, it will default to Delete."},{"kind":"p","text":"PersistentVolumes that are created manually and managed via a StorageClass will have whatever reclaim policy they were assigned at creation."}],"lemmas":{"storages":["storage","storages"],"classes":["class","classes"],"classed":["classed","class"],"documents":["document","documents"],"documenting":["documenting","document"],"documented":["documented","document"],"describes":["describes","describe"],"described":["described","describe"],"describing":["describing","describe"],"concepts":["concept","concepts"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"familiarities":["familiarity","familiarities"